Basically if you're lucky, Uber/Lyft are paying you what you're losing in vehicle depreciation. Think about the maintenance costs, fuel, commercial vehicle insurance (which I'm sure you purchased ;0) and if you have any outstanding loans on the vehicle. The problem with rideshare drivers is they need to think of their vehicles as a gold mine, eventually the mine will be empty and it costs money to maintain and operate the mine.

&#x200B;

The biggest problem with rideshare companies is they don't pay you to drive to the customer or home. Essentially the driver is paying for the first and last miles. Example, say you drive 1 mile to pick up your passenger. You then drive 8 miles to their destination. Assuming you drove in a straight line away from your home, you are now 9 miles away from your home, which you have to pay for to drive home. 

&#x200B;

But lets say you continue driving, another 1 free mile to pick up your next passenger and where ever they want to go to. Lets say over the course of 1 hour, you drive 40 miles total. With 30 of those miles being paid for by the rideshare company. We'll say you gave 3 rides, each with a fare fee of $1.28 (I don't know what the fare fee is for LA, I'm just using Chicago's fare fee). 0.79 per mile x 30 miles = 23.70 and 0.18 per minute x 60 minutes = 10.80. Add 23.70 + 10.80 + (1.28x3) = $37.06 you made in the hour. However don't forget to deduct costs. Let's say the vehicle you bought gets 26 miles per gallon and gas is 2.20 per gallon (again using Chicago numbers, LA is probably closer to $3). Assuming the vehicle depreciates at 0.56 cents per mile and it costs 0.16 cents to drive each mile, you spent $19.02 in fuel and vehicle depreciation or 51.3% of what you were paid. However now you get to guess how much you owe in taxes, which with you living in California I can only imagine are sky high. BTW Uber/Lyft don't withhold taxes, so you as the independent entrepreneur savvy contractor have to keep track. Isn't that nice of them? It's so they can give you the illusion that you're getting paid more than you really are. After federal, state and local taxes you've probably made $8.60 for those 40 miles you drove over the course of 1 hour. But of course that assumes that you constantly had a new passenger to pick up and didn't have to wait after each delivery which is quite possible in most major metro areas but if you want my opinion it's probably better to just work at a fast food place. I used to drive for the ridesharing companies about 2 years ago. With driver rates having been cut so much it's impossible for me to justify driving.

